# MECHANISM PIN — a KB may close its own base vocabulary.
#
# Fixture INLINE (mechanism pin): the subject is the ENGINE's closure, not any one KB.
#
# WHY. `derived_only("false")` says `false` may never be ASSERTED — but it says nothing
# about what may. Any corpus name at all still entered a knowledge base, fail-open, so a
# document claiming "the record has exactly these entries, and a further one cannot be
# written" was claiming something the engine did not check. `admits("<relation>")` is the
# missing dual: it names what this KB takes as evidence and refuses the rest at assert
# time, with the same message shape and the same atomicity `derived_only` uses.
#
# The two together close the extensional side completely: `derived_only(R)` says R may not
# be asserted, `admits(R)` says only R and its fellows may. Neither alone is enough.

# ── THE DECLARATION BLOCK, WHICH MUST COME FIRST ─────────────────────────────
admits("person").
admits("home").

# ── ADMITTED VOCABULARY ASSERTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────
:accept
person(Adam).

:accept
home(Adam).

# ── EVERYTHING ELSE IS REFUSED, FAIL-CLOSED ──────────────────────────────────
# Not linted, not warned: rejected, and the whole assertion unwinds atomically.
:refuse reasoning /`rich` is not admitted vocabulary/
rich(Adam).

:refuse reasoning /not admitted vocabulary/
banana(Adam).

# ── THE CLOSURE IS EXTENSIONAL ONLY ──────────────────────────────────────────
# A rule may still CONCLUDE a relation outside the admitted set — the dual of
# `derived_only`, which permits derivation and refuses assertion. Closing the base
# vocabulary must not silently close the derived one.
:accept
all $x: person($x) -> prisoner($x).

? prisoner(Adam).
# => TRUE

# ...and the same relation still cannot be asserted directly.
:refuse reasoning /not admitted vocabulary/
prisoner(Bela).

# ── THE DECLARATION RELATIONS STAY WRITABLE ──────────────────────────────────
# `admits` and `derived_only` are exempt, and must be: a KB that closed its vocabulary
# still has to be able to declare, and `admits("admits")` could not otherwise store its
# own declaration.
:accept
derived_only("prisoner").

:accept
admits("free").

# A relation admitted LATER is assertable from then on — the block is not frozen, it is
# only required to precede the facts.
:accept
free(Adam).

:expect-pins 10
